    "id" : 32,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.919, 99.288, 524.604, 196.416 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "where matrix Rm×n is the historical returns of asset i on its i-th column over m observation periods, see [7]. The observation data contained in this matrix is used to estimate two important parameters in MV model: expected return and covariance matrix. If the number of the chosen observations m is small compared to the number of assets, then the sample covariance matrix becomes ill-conditioned, one suﬀers from the over-ﬁtting problem [12, 3]. In this case, a high regularization promotes the sparsity of the solutions and mitigates over-ﬁtting. On the other hand, if the number of samples is large enough, then the model obtained is truth and a small amount of regularization is reasonable. Based on these, we regard m1 as a factor of the initial regularization parameter λ0."
  }, {
